1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,534
Can you show me Git submodules?

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:14,200
A submodule embeds another Git repository inside yours, pinned to one exact commit.

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:22,211
Useful for sharing a library, a chart or a set of templates between repositories; also a source of confusion,

4
00:00:22,211 --> 00:00:26,474
because cloning, updating and committing need extra steps.

5
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:28,384
We go through all of them.

6
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:34,533
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

7
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:38,491
Add the shared repository as a submodule:

8
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:41,482
Mode 160000 and type commit:

9
00:00:41,482 --> 00:00:48,303
the main repository stores a commit ID of the other repository, not its files.

10
00:00:50,067 --> 00:00:51,847
Let me try something.

11
00:00:51,847 --> 00:00:55,831
Grace clones the cafe repository the usual way:

12
00:00:56,367 --> 00:01:01,274
shared/ is empty and git submodule status shows a leading -:

13
00:01:01,274 --> 00:01:05,201
the submodule is registered but not initialised.

14
00:01:05,201 --> 00:01:08,799
A plain git clone does not fetch submodules.

15
00:01:08,799 --> 00:01:14,116
Anything that needs shared/tax.txt (a build, a deployment) fails.

16
00:01:15,867 --> 00:01:16,817
Next time:

17
00:01:16,817 --> 00:01:19,761
git clone --recurse-submodules.

18
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:22,213
Got it.

19
00:01:22,213 --> 00:01:24,965
What do I need to remember?

20
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:34,775
A submodule = another repository pinned to one commit (.gitmodules + a gitlink, mode 160000).

21
00:01:35,333 --> 00:01:40,923
Clone with --recurse-submodules, or git submodule update --init afterwards.

22
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:42,590
Updating means:

23
00:01:42,590 --> 00:01:47,606
move the submodule, then commit the new pin in the main repository.

24
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:52,552
I keep hearing about Git worktrees.

25
00:01:52,552 --> 00:01:54,281
What is it, really?

26
00:01:54,833 --> 00:02:00,063
A worktree is an extra working directory attached to the same repository.

27
00:02:00,063 --> 00:02:04,934
Instead of stashing your half-finished feature to fix an urgent bug,

28
00:02:04,934 --> 00:02:10,449
you open the bug fix in a second folder, on another branch, at the same time.

29
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:13,673
One repository, several checked-out branches.

30
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:19,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

31
00:02:20,367 --> 00:02:25,893
Open a second working directory for the hotfix, without touching the current one:

32
00:02:25,893 --> 00:02:27,326
Fix and commit there:

33
00:02:27,867 --> 00:02:29,911
Fix and commit there:

34
00:02:30,467 --> 00:02:34,882
Back in the feature folder, the uncommitted work is untouched,

35
00:02:34,882 --> 00:02:38,301
and the new commit is visible (same repository):

36
00:02:40,067 --> 00:02:41,671
Let me try something.

37
00:02:41,671 --> 00:02:45,566
Try to check out main in the main worktree as well:

38
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:46,562
fatal:

39
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:51,110
'main' is already used by worktree at '…/lesson-87-hotfix':

40
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:56,043
two folders on the same branch would each move it independently.

41
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:59,434
Git allows each branch in one worktree only.

42
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:05,855
Finish with the hotfix worktree and remove it; then main is free:

43
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,313
Got it.

44
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:11,065
What do I need to remember?

45
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:17,489
A worktree is another working directory of the same repository, on another branch.

46
00:03:18,033 --> 00:03:22,643
No stashing or second clone needed to work on two things at once.

47
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:28,355
One branch per worktree; git worktree remove / prune to clean up.

48
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:32,659
How does Git LFS work?

49
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,289
Git keeps every version of every file forever, in every clone.

50
00:03:38,289 --> 00:03:47,234
For large binaries (images, videos, datasets, model files, installers) that makes repositories huge and slow.

51
00:03:47,234 --> 00:03:56,344
Git LFS (Large File Storage) stores those files on a separate server and keeps only small pointer files in Git.

52
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:02,499
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

53
00:04:03,033 --> 00:04:09,222
Tell LFS which files to manage; the rule is stored in .gitattributes (committed):

54
00:04:09,222 --> 00:04:11,973
Add a 2 MB file and commit as usual:

55
00:04:12,533 --> 00:04:15,330
Add a 2 MB file and commit as usual:

56
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:18,438
What Git actually stored is the pointer:

57
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,776
What Git actually stored is the pointer:

58
00:04:21,776 --> 00:04:29,409
A push uploads the content to the LFS store (on the server, next to the Git objects), then the commits to Git:

59
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:32,744
Let me try something.

60
00:04:32,744 --> 00:04:40,106
Someone commits a large file of a type that is not tracked yet, then adds the LFS rule afterwards:

61
00:04:40,667 --> 00:04:42,788
The blob is 3,000,000 bytes:

62
00:04:42,788 --> 00:04:45,667
a full file inside Git, not a pointer.

63
00:04:45,667 --> 00:04:53,546
A tracking rule only applies to files added after it; the existing commit already contains the big blob,

64
00:04:53,546 --> 00:04:58,244
and pushing it would put it in Git history forever (on GitHub:

65
00:04:58,244 --> 00:04:59,986
rejected above 100 MB).

66
00:05:01,733 --> 00:05:06,353
The commits are not pushed yet, so history can be rewritten:

67
00:05:06,353 --> 00:05:10,048
move every CSV in the unpushed commits into LFS.

68
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:12,513
Got it.

69
00:05:12,513 --> 00:05:15,264
What do I need to remember?

70
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:21,115
LFS keeps large binaries out of Git history; Git stores pointers.

71
00:05:21,667 --> 00:05:26,926
git lfs track before adding the files; commit .gitattributes.

72
00:05:27,467 --> 00:05:29,994
Large files already committed:

73
00:05:29,994 --> 00:05:33,531
git lfs migrate import (rewrites history).
